1
00:00:00,251 --> 00:00:03,142
♪ [music] ♪

2
00:00:14,071 --> 00:00:15,362
- [Tyler] In the previous video,

3
00:00:15,362 --> 00:00:17,619
we discussed how
asymmetric information

4
00:00:17,619 --> 00:00:19,491
can lead to moral hazard,

5
00:00:19,492 --> 00:00:21,712
which can lead 
to people being ripped off,

6
00:00:21,712 --> 00:00:24,972
which in turn can lead 
to markets breaking down.

7
00:00:24,972 --> 00:00:27,831
Now, there are two types 
of potential solutions

8
00:00:27,831 --> 00:00:29,870
to moral hazard problems.

9
00:00:29,871 --> 00:00:33,292
First, since the problem 
is asymmetric information,

10
00:00:33,292 --> 00:00:36,623
you can try to make 
information less asymmetric.

11
00:00:36,623 --> 00:00:38,893
If both parties 
have similar information,

12
00:00:38,893 --> 00:00:42,353
then one party cannot 
so easily exploit the other.

13
00:00:42,353 --> 00:00:45,693
Second, if you can't get rid 
of the information asymmetry,

14
00:00:45,693 --> 00:00:49,403
you can try to reduce the incentive
of the agent to exploit

15
00:00:49,403 --> 00:00:52,441
his or her informational advantage.

16
00:00:52,441 --> 00:00:54,694
One example
that you're already familiar with

17
00:00:54,694 --> 00:00:57,182
works along both
of these dimensions:

18
00:00:57,182 --> 00:00:58,792
user ratings.

19
00:00:58,792 --> 00:01:01,083
You can get ratings
for businesses at sites

20
00:01:01,083 --> 00:01:03,182
such as Yelp and Angie's List,

21
00:01:03,182 --> 00:01:07,031
and for products 
at places such as Amazon.com.

22
00:01:07,031 --> 00:01:10,713
Reviews give you more information 
about a product or service,

23
00:01:10,713 --> 00:01:12,791
and they help balance
the information

24
00:01:12,791 --> 00:01:14,463
between buyer and seller,

25
00:01:14,463 --> 00:01:18,055
thereby mitigating issues 
of moral hazard.

26
00:01:18,055 --> 00:01:21,082
Additionally, the existence 
of these review sites

27
00:01:21,082 --> 00:01:23,832
changes the incentives 
for the sellers.

28
00:01:23,832 --> 00:01:26,492
In our previous example 
of the auto mechanic

29
00:01:26,492 --> 00:01:29,922
we sketched out how your incentive 
to get your car repaired

30
00:01:29,922 --> 00:01:32,243
might conflict
with the mechanic's incentive

31
00:01:32,244 --> 00:01:34,932
to milk you for extra money.

32
00:01:34,932 --> 00:01:38,871
However, the mechanic also
has to think about her reputation,

33
00:01:38,871 --> 00:01:41,622
which is what leads 
to future business.

34
00:01:41,622 --> 00:01:43,352
Previously, you might have told,

35
00:01:43,352 --> 00:01:46,092
well, just a few friends 
that you got ripped off.

36
00:01:46,093 --> 00:01:49,114
Now, today, you can write 
an online review

37
00:01:49,114 --> 00:01:52,792
and reach a much larger audience, 
maybe thousands of people.

38
00:01:52,792 --> 00:01:56,464
A mechanic's online reputation 
is important to her success

39
00:01:56,464 --> 00:01:59,823
and provides an incentive 
for her not to try to exploit

40
00:01:59,823 --> 00:02:02,173
her informational advantage.

41
00:02:02,173 --> 00:02:06,113
While review sites are great, 
the market is continually evolving,

42
00:02:06,113 --> 00:02:07,943
and some sellers try to fight back

43
00:02:07,943 --> 00:02:11,525
by faking or manipulating 
reviews or ratings.

44
00:02:11,525 --> 00:02:13,144
There's some obvious examples,

45
00:02:13,144 --> 00:02:16,321
like favorably reviewing 
your friend's book on Amazon

46
00:02:16,322 --> 00:02:18,295
if your friend reviews yours.

47
00:02:18,295 --> 00:02:20,824
But there are more 
subtle examples as well.

48
00:02:20,824 --> 00:02:24,485
For instance, some universities 
invite lots of applicants,

49
00:02:24,485 --> 00:02:26,258
knowing they will reject them,

50
00:02:26,258 --> 00:02:29,883
just hoping to boost 
their rating for exclusivity.

51
00:02:29,883 --> 00:02:33,045
Reviews are more trustworthy 
if they come from third parties

52
00:02:33,045 --> 00:02:35,964
who do not have incentives 
to bias the review

53
00:02:35,964 --> 00:02:37,674
one way or the other.

54
00:02:37,674 --> 00:02:40,333
The magazine Consumer Reports, 
for example,

55
00:02:40,333 --> 00:02:42,894
has a reputation
for honest reporting

56
00:02:42,894 --> 00:02:46,364
because it's a non-profit
and it accepts no advertisements

57
00:02:46,364 --> 00:02:49,283
and no funding 
from commercial sources.

58
00:02:49,283 --> 00:02:50,773
But there's another problem --

59
00:02:50,773 --> 00:02:54,844
it's hard to exclude people 
from produced information.

60
00:02:54,844 --> 00:02:58,045
Information is also 
what we call nonrival --

61
00:02:58,046 --> 00:03:00,334
nonexcludable and nonrival.

62
00:03:00,334 --> 00:03:02,703
Do those property sound familiar?

63
00:03:02,703 --> 00:03:05,643
Yes, information
has the characteristics

64
00:03:05,643 --> 00:03:07,523
of a public good.

65
00:03:07,523 --> 00:03:10,362
Click to go back 
and review if you need.

66
00:03:10,363 --> 00:03:14,633
It's hard for Consumer Reports 
to keep the information it provides

67
00:03:14,633 --> 00:03:17,642
to only those people 
who buy the magazine.

68
00:03:17,643 --> 00:03:21,382
If you hear that Consumer Reports 
says that brand X of television

69
00:03:21,382 --> 00:03:22,721
is the very best,

70
00:03:22,721 --> 00:03:24,513
well you might use that information

71
00:03:24,513 --> 00:03:27,804
even if you didn't buy 
the magazine and pay for it.

72
00:03:27,804 --> 00:03:31,033
In this case, we would say 
that you are free riding,

73
00:03:31,033 --> 00:03:33,305
using the benefits
of their research

74
00:03:33,305 --> 00:03:36,764
and testing without contributing 
to paying the costs.

75
00:03:37,394 --> 00:03:41,627
Free riding is a problem, however, 
not because it's unfair or unjust,

76
00:03:41,628 --> 00:03:44,384
but because it means 
that information provision

77
00:03:44,384 --> 00:03:47,223
may be under-provided 
in the first place.

78
00:03:47,223 --> 00:03:50,583
As a result, there are fewer 
trustworthy third parties

79
00:03:50,583 --> 00:03:53,122
reviewing products and services.

80
00:03:53,123 --> 00:03:56,415
So, review sites help solve 
moral hazard issues

81
00:03:56,415 --> 00:03:59,963
by both balancing the information 
between buyer and seller,

82
00:03:59,963 --> 00:04:03,433
and reducing the incentives 
for the more informed party

83
00:04:03,434 --> 00:04:06,363
to exploit
an informational advantage.

84
00:04:07,843 --> 00:04:09,014
There are other approaches

85
00:04:09,014 --> 00:04:11,891
that work solely
by modifying the incentives

86
00:04:11,891 --> 00:04:15,365
of those people
with the informational advantages.

87
00:04:15,365 --> 00:04:16,716
Let's take two examples

88
00:04:16,716 --> 00:04:18,624
that might seem
completely different,

89
00:04:18,624 --> 00:04:21,384
but are actually, conceptually, 
pretty similar:

90
00:04:21,384 --> 00:04:25,564
house inspections,
and second opinions from doctors.

91
00:04:25,564 --> 00:04:28,413
When you buy a house, 
you typically get a home inspector

92
00:04:28,413 --> 00:04:31,964
to check out the house 
and identify potential problems.

93
00:04:31,964 --> 00:04:33,535
By law in many states,

94
00:04:33,536 --> 00:04:36,723
these home inspectors 
cannot also sell services

95
00:04:36,723 --> 00:04:40,054
to fix any problems
they've identified.

96
00:04:40,054 --> 00:04:41,935
This changes their incentives.

97
00:04:41,935 --> 00:04:43,684
No longer do they have the incentive

98
00:04:43,684 --> 00:04:46,085
to overstate
potential problem issues

99
00:04:46,085 --> 00:04:47,595
to inflate the bill.

100
00:04:47,595 --> 00:04:50,614
Similarly, when you go to a doctor 
for a second opinion,

101
00:04:50,614 --> 00:04:53,525
that doctor is simply 
diagnosing the problem,

102
00:04:53,525 --> 00:04:55,707
but not doing the actual treatment.

103
00:04:55,707 --> 00:04:57,315
Just like with the home inspector,

104
00:04:57,315 --> 00:05:00,544
this eliminates the incentive 
to run up the bill.

105
00:05:01,254 --> 00:05:04,835
Both of these examples 
split the diagnosis of the issue

106
00:05:04,835 --> 00:05:07,442
from the actual work 
to address the problem.

107
00:05:07,442 --> 00:05:09,875
So while the information 
is still asymmetric,

108
00:05:09,875 --> 00:05:14,184
there is less of an incentive 
to exploit that asymmetry.

109
00:05:14,184 --> 00:05:16,504
By the time you're ready 
to fix the issue,

110
00:05:16,504 --> 00:05:18,833
this separate diagnosis by an expert

111
00:05:18,833 --> 00:05:21,523
allows you to have
much more information.

112
00:05:21,523 --> 00:05:26,196
Maybe now, almost as much 
as the service provider in some ways.

113
00:05:26,196 --> 00:05:28,613
This video has covered 
the various solutions

114
00:05:28,613 --> 00:05:30,175
to moral hazard issues

115
00:05:30,175 --> 00:05:33,215
in situations 
of asymmetric information.

116
00:05:33,216 --> 00:05:34,365
In the next video,

117
00:05:34,365 --> 00:05:36,404
we'll take up
a different kind of response

118
00:05:36,404 --> 00:05:38,495
to the asymmetric
information problem,

119
00:05:38,495 --> 00:05:40,324
namely signaling.

120
00:05:40,324 --> 00:05:41,935
See you then.

121
